WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:02.560
Hi, I'm Nataraj Nagaratnam

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.440
and I'm from IBM Cloud.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.940
Traditionally when you deploy an application

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:11.300
you have the entire data center, the servers that you run - you're responsible for all of it.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.740
In the cloud model that's a shared responsibility

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:23.000
between you and the cloud provider.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.720
In a shared responsibility model you need to rethink security

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.760
on what your responsibility is and what  the cloud provider's responsibility is.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:34.080
Let's take platform-as-a-service (PaaS) as an example.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.780
When you look at PaaS, you're building applications,

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.740
migrating data to the cloud

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.280
and building applications running them on the cloud.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:46.520
So, you're responsible for securing the applications, the workload and the data

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:51.180
while the cloud provider is responsible for managing the security of the platform.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:56.840
So that it's compliant, it's secured from the perspective of network,

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:03.080
the platform on down in terms of managing the containers and the runtime and isolation,

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:07.040
so that you have your own space within the platform.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:11.360
Whereas if you are adopting and migrating workloads to the cloud

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.260
and you're using infrastructure-as-a-service (IaaS),

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:20.100
then the cloud provider manages hypervisor on down if you are using virtual servers

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:25.580
or, if you are using bare metal, then you can completely control everything on up

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:29.400
from the operating system, the virtual servers that you run,

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.120
and the data you bring it on.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.860
So it's very important to understand
the adoption model whether you're

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:44.460
consuming IaaS or PaaS, or if you're
consuming SaaS where the cloud provider

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.570
manages all the applications and the
security offered and you worry about the

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.560
data that you bring in and plan accordingly.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:56.399
So that's a very important thing because it's part of understanding your

00:01:56.399 --> 00:02:01.710
responsibility in ultimately managing the risk and compliance of the workloads

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:03.560
of the data that you bring to cloud.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.380
Now let's talk about architecture.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:09.000
When you build applications and migrate
applications and modernize your apps

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:10.820
- let's start with data.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:19.040
With all the risk that you deal with, and the kind of data matters. Is it confidential data, is it public data, or

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.600
sensitive data that may deal with private information.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:29.040
Consider all those factors and make a secure design around what your data security architecture should be.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:35.390
Make sure you have data at rest encryption so that the data is

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:39.590
always encrypted whether you use a
database as a service, object store as a

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:44.150
service, or other ways to store data
like block storage. Encryption is for

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:49.670
amateurs, and we think about key management for professionals. So having more

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:54.320
control of your keys provide you the
ability in the context of shared

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.610
responsibility model that you own your
data you have complete control of your

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:04.010
data. So as you think about key
management make sure you have an

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:07.370
approach to think about if you are
bringing confidential data you want to

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:12.170
bring your own keys may be sensitive
data you want to keep your own keys. So

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:18.350
that how much control of the keys you
have and the hardware security module in

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:22.370
which the key processing the encryption
decryption operations happen more

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:28.610
control you have more responsibility
that you can take on. So encryption at

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:36.769
data at rest, data in motion, as it
comes from services to data stores or

00:03:36.769 --> 00:03:42.140
applications so that as you think about
data coming out of the way your requests

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:49.820
and API requests coming all the way data
in motion. And in the new world we need

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:53.000
to start thinking about when the
application is actually processing the

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:58.370
data that is going to be data in its
memory. So you can actually start to

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:02.720
protect data using hardware based
technologies where you can protect

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:10.280
in-memory data as well. So that when it
is in use and in memory by the

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:14.450
applications you can protect it. So take
a holistic approach to data protection

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:19.760
at rest, in motion, in use with full
control of your keys. It can be bring

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:27.730
your own keys, or even better push the
boundary with keep your own keys.

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:34.100
The application that serves the data
it's not only about which application

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:40.100
needs to have access make sure the data
access is on an only by need basis. Do

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:44.660
not open up your data services to the
whole world, be it network access or

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:49.370
everybody to access the data, make sure
you exactly know which applications need

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:54.970
to access or which users need to access
the data to run your cloud applications.

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:59.900
From an application viewpoint make sure
there are no vulnerabilities in your

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:06.710
application so scan your applications, so
I have an App SEC application security

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:10.430
approach so that you can do dynamic
scanning or static scanning of your

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:14.479
application before you deploy it into
the production, and in the cloud-native

00:05:14.479 --> 00:05:19.190
environment you're deploying container
images so you can scan your images, you

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:24.919
can scan it for vulnerabilities before
you deploy and set your policies so that

00:05:24.919 --> 00:05:30.380
you only have secured images in
production any time and if there is any

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:34.640
vulnerability in the new world you don't
need to patch these systems you just

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:38.900
spin up a new container and off you go.
So that's the beauty of a cloud-native

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:44.150
approach that you have security built in
in every step. So at a container level

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:50.479
and the applications that serves the
business logic you can start to protect

00:05:50.479 --> 00:05:57.229
it. Then when you look at the users
coming in you want to manage access in

00:05:57.229 --> 00:06:05.229
terms of who the user is and what from
there they are coming from. So identity

00:06:05.229 --> 00:06:09.830
you need to make sure who the user is
or which service it is based on the

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:14.750
identity of those services or users so
that you can manage access control to your

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:21.289
application or data and also from the
perspective of network access you want

00:06:21.289 --> 00:06:28.430
to make sure only authorized users can
get in and if there are intruders out

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:33.889
there you can make sure you can set it
up so that they are prevented from

00:06:33.889 --> 00:06:40.340
accessing your application and your data
in the cloud, be it through Web

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:43.870
Application Firewall-ing, network access control or

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:47.780
denial-of-service distributed,
denial-of-service protection and have

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:53.270
intelligence built into these network
protection as well. So both identity and

00:06:53.270 --> 00:07:04.729
network. In essence, you are protecting
your data, you need to manage access to

00:07:04.729 --> 00:07:08.960
your apps and the workload on the data
that you have deployed on the cloud. You

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.669
need to have a continuous security
monitoring so that you know at any point

00:07:13.669 --> 00:07:18.409
whether you're compliant to your
policies, you can watch out for threats

00:07:18.409 --> 00:07:22.340
that you need to manage, having an
approach and set of tools to manage

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:28.460
security and complaints posture is very
important. So gaining insights about your

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:37.969
posture, compliance, and threats. So from
your deployment environment you can

00:07:37.969 --> 00:07:45.020
garner information, it can be security
events, audit logs, flow logs from network

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:51.169
or system that can be fed in so that you
can figure out what your posture and

00:07:51.169 --> 00:07:54.740
complaints and threats are, and that is not only important for you to gain insight

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:59.750
you need to have actionable intelligence
so that you can start to remediate. You

00:07:59.750 --> 00:08:02.900
may figure out there's a vulnerability, a
container image that you have deployed

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:06.680
is vulnerable so you can re-spin the
container so you can remediate and spin

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:10.849
up a new container. There may be a
particular access from a network that

00:08:10.849 --> 00:08:15.770
seems to be coming in from a suspicious
network IP address so we can block that.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:20.000
So the ability to gain visibility and
insights and having that insights and

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:25.370
turn it into actionable intelligence and
remediate is very important. So let's

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:30.589
talk about DevOps. DevOps is about
development and operations. Traditionally

00:08:30.589 --> 00:08:33.949
we think about okay, there's an
application team that is doing the

00:08:33.949 --> 00:08:38.060
design and architecture, who are building
code, and then you throw it over the wall

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:43.909
for the enterprise security team to
secure it and manage it. That should be

00:08:43.909 --> 00:08:50.480
rethought, fundamentally it's not just
about Dev and Ops, but security need

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:55.060
to be a forethought not an afterthought.
So it should become

00:08:55.060 --> 00:09:01.220
SecDevOps approach to the way you
build, manage, and run your applications.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:05.959
So you need to embed security into the
entire lifecycle, what we call shift left,

00:09:05.959 --> 00:09:09.920
not only you manage security but shift
left through the entire process you need

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:15.500
to have a secure design, so as you plan
as you design and say what kind of data

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:18.829
am I going to put what level of
classification what kind of applications

00:09:18.829 --> 00:09:23.959
am I building, is it container based, is
it a workload that I'm migrating, take

00:09:23.959 --> 00:09:27.740
that into account and what integrations
you need to do so that you can plan it

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:33.200
and architect it. Then as you build it
embed security as part of that process.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:36.829
So you have security aware applications, for example you may want to

00:09:36.829 --> 00:09:40.700
encrypt data of your sensitive data, you
may want to encrypt the data from your

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:46.760
applications before you even you store
into a data store. So secure build and

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:51.709
you manage security as part of SecDevops as you have secure design and

00:09:51.709 --> 00:09:57.019
architecture you pass on that and build
secure applications and deploy and

00:09:57.019 --> 00:10:00.050
manage security in a continuous
fashion and then you have a closed loop

00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:04.579
so that whatever you find you may need
to remediate or rearchitect your

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:08.460
application or implement certain things
as threats landscape evolve.

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:09.880
Thanks for watching this video.

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:11.940
